Steps to Create a Kubernetes Cluster

## Using EC2 Instance
- Launch EC2 (Ubuntu, `t2.medium`, open SSH/HTTP, 25GB memory).
- Connect:
  ```bash
  sudo su
  apt update -y
  ```

 Install kubectl
```bash
curl -LO "https://dl.k8s.io/release/$(curl -L -s https://dl.k8s.io/release/stable.txt)/bin/linux/amd64/kubectl"
chmod +x kubectl
mkdir -p ~/.local/bin
mv ./kubectl ~/.local/bin/kubectl
kubectl version --client
```

Install eksctl
```bash
ARCH=amd64
PLATFORM=$(uname -s)_$ARCH
curl -sLO "https://github.com/eksctl-io/eksctl/releases/latest/download/eksctl_$PLATFORM.tar.gz"
tar -xzf eksctl_$PLATFORM.tar.gz -C /tmp && rm eksctl_$PLATFORM.tar.gz
sudo mv /tmp/eksctl /usr/local/bin
apt install unzip -y
```

 Install AWS CLI
```bash
curl "https://awscli.amazonaws.com/awscli-exe-linux-x86_64.zip" -o "awscliv2.zip"
unzip awscliv2.zip
sudo ./aws/install
aws --version
```

Configure IAM Access Keys
- Create IAM user → Attach **AdministratorAccess** policy → Create access key → Save `.csv`
- In EC2 terminal:
  ```bash
  aws configure
  ```

---

 Create Kubernetes Cluster with `eksctl`
```bash
eksctl create cluster \
--name blue-cluster \
--region sa-east-1 \
--zone sa-east-1a,sa-east-1b \
--nodegroup-name red-node \
--node-type t2.medium \
--nodes 2
```
> This takes about 10 minutes to spin up.

